model {
  extend basalt {
    pool = module "Pool app" {
      #basalt #v1 #deep
      description "One private app instance per person per organization, run by the tools service. The pool owner's membership-scoped Home readings feed the host's generic allowance observations, preserving reported windows, quantities, periods, source times and expiring balances. Observation endpoints remain owner-only; members keep the existing health path. Hosted reset actions take a saved account and explicit pool, require current account authority and that pool's owner, and keep native or retained subscription backend references on the server. Browser spending rejects API credentials; agent spending requires framework approval. Fresh catalog and receipt reads never spend or run follow-ups. The owner can read the latest durable attempt after losing a reply or client draft; that read preserves its original credit and request ID, result and retry deadline without running provider or follow-up work. Unknown attempts remain blocked after the provider retry deadline. Accounts uses the host's native check status with exception-only pool badges; idle freshness, zero reset counts and internal window IDs are omitted. A lab's identity colour comes from one shared mapping, used by provider icons, healthy usage bars and pool usage chart series; low, critical and stale readings keep their status colours, and a stale reading names itself. Usage screen; Usage shows tokens and API-equivalent cost per model over 30 days, the whole pool for an authorized manager who can use it and only the caller's own otherwise. Before the native-policy handoff the recorded owner retains the existing management rule. People set up and list their computers on the tools gateway's Connect page, which asks the pool Worker for a signed setup intent through this app's computerContext query. App screens embed on isolated opaque same-site origins; live routing acceptance is pending. When its first load succeeds, the pool marks the committed account view, including an authorized empty result, once per mount as basalt:pool-accounts-ready and posts only that name, contract version 2 and a snapshot boolean to the configured host origin. The parent probe accepts only those three fields, a boolean snapshot value, the expected origin and the exact iframe window. A failed first load excludes the document from readiness measurement even after recovery. A snapshot commits before the single fresh follow-up starts. The readiness mark detail and parent boolean describe the same first committed view; legacy views without reading metadata remain live. Document-load readiness and usage readiness remain separate."
      metadata { code "apps/pool"; libraries "apps|react|react-dom"; bindings "ctx=basalt.router" }
    }
  }
}
